<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
<Document>
  <name>Tuesday Tokyo · May 26</name>
  <description><![CDATA[Tsukiji dawn · Yanaka shitamachi · Kokekokko 19:00 (yakitori)]]></description>

  <Style id="locked"><IconStyle><scale>1.3</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/ylw-stars.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="major"><IconStyle><scale>1.1</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/red-circle.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="bonus"><IconStyle><scale>0.9</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/blu-blank.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="station"><IconStyle><scale>0.9</scale><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/shapes/rail.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>

  <Folder>
    <name>LOCKED — confirmed bookings / hard anchors</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Syamo Sumibi Kokekokko (Yakitori)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>19:00 sharp · 2 adults · counter seating</b><br><br><b>BOOKING:</b> ✅ CONCIERGE CONFIRMED · Mr. Gavin Mead · 2 adults · counter · Nishishinjuku B2F · cashless cc typical<br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Specialist shamo (heritage gamecock) yakitori counter in the basement of Nishi-Shinjuku Itoyama Tower. Cooks heritage Japanese gamecock — slower-growing, free-roaming, longer-fibered muscle = deeper flavor than commodity chicken. Part-by-part skewer omakase including organ meats and tare-glazed thigh-skin.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Pure single-tradition cuisine, counter intimacy with the grill master in front of you, ingredient-led — exactly your pattern. NOT the streetside Omoide Yokocho stalls (touristy, smoke-blown); this is the serious basement counter version.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Heritage Japanese shamo chicken; part-by-part skewer omakase; charcoal-grill technique; counter only.<br><br>Address: 1-1-1 Nishishinjuku B2F, Shinjuku-ku · 新宿区⻄新宿1-1-1 B2F<br><br><b>↪ Shinjuku station west exit ~7 min walk through Nishi-Shinjuku office canyon · BE ON TIME</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#locked</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.6985,35.6929,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>MAJOR — the planned spine</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tsukiji Outer Market (08:00 arrival)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>08:00 · alive-and-humming hour · ~2 hrs · cash mostly</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> The outer market of the historic Tsukiji wholesale fish market (the inner wholesale operation moved to Toyosu in 2018, but the OUTER market — the food-and-knife retail and standing-eating-counters lane — stayed intact and is thriving). 5 narrow blocks of vendors selling sushi, tamagoyaki, uni, scallops grilled over coals, oysters, sliced maguro cheek, fresh wasabi, ceramics, knives.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Karolina's market thread at its purest. Mid-morning breakfast = grazing your way through a 400-vendor lane of Tokyo's best raw materials. Original Monday plan; shifted to today.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Standing-counter sushi at the source (the boats unload at 03:00; the best stuff hits the outer-market counters by 07:00). Cash mostly. The energy is the experience.<br><br>Address: 5 Chome, Tsukiji, Chuo-ku<br><br><b>↪ From hotel: 8 min walk to Hamamatsuchō → Yamanote 1 stop to Shimbashi → Hibiya line 2 stops to Tsukiji · ~22 min · OR ~25 min walk along the bay</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7705,35.6654,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Sushi-Dai / Daiwa Sushi (Tsukiji standing sushi)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>~08:30 if you want sushi for breakfast · queues form</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Two famous standing-sushi counters in the Tsukiji Outer Market. Sushi-Dai is the historic name (relocated post-2018), Daiwa is its near-twin. Both serve omakase nigiri from the day's auction, 10-15 pieces, ~¥4-5k.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Sushi-for-breakfast at the source. The lines are real (45-90 min) — go EARLY (08:00 arrival = ~30 min wait) or skip in favor of standing tamagoyaki + uni at vendors.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Daiwa is generally faster line than Sushi-Dai; both pour first cup of green tea immediately upon seating; tuna cuts include otoro on most omakase.<br><br><b>↪ Inside the Tsukiji Outer Market area</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7708,35.6651,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Tsukiji vendor crawl (grazing, ~90 min)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>08:00-10:00 · grazing pace · cash</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Walk the lanes once before committing. Must-eat stalls: Yamacho or Marutomo for tamagoyaki-on-a-stick (¥150), Marutake for uni in the shell, oyster bars for raw oysters, scallop skewer over coals at Kitsune, maguro cheek skewers, fresh wasabi root from Yamamoto Yamasa-en. Most stalls cash only.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Grazing beats committing to one sit-down meal. You hit 6-8 vendors in 90 min, eat a full breakfast across them, total ~¥3-4k each.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Tamagoyaki on a stick is the iconic Tsukiji bite; uni-in-the-shell is the splurge; ceramics shops scattered through the lanes if you want a small pottery piece.<br><br><b>↪ Just walk the lanes</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7702,35.6654,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Yanaka Ginza shopping street (10:30 arrival)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>10:30 · old-Tokyo shitamachi · ~90 min walk</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> A 170m covered shopping street in Yanaka — one of central Tokyo's few preserved shitamachi (old-town low-city) neighborhoods. Survived WWII firebombing intact. ~70 small shops: dried fish, croquettes, dorayaki, rice crackers, ceramics, small antiques. Cats everywhere (Yanaka is famously cat-friendly).<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Old Tokyo that doesn't exist elsewhere in central Tokyo. The wooden buildings, the cats, the family-run shops that haven't changed in 50 years. Karolina's market+craft thread, Gavin's pre-war thread — both fed here.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The Yuyake Dandan stairs at the entrance (sunset overlook); the seven lucky cats hidden in shop signs; Kanekichi-en tea shop; Hibikiya for dorayaki; Niku no Suzuki for ¥100 menchi-katsu (deep-fried beef cake) eaten standing.<br><br>Address: Yanaka 3-chome area, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ From Tsukiji: Hibiya line to Roppongi → Chiyoda line to Sendagi (~25 min) · OR JR to Nippori + 5 min walk</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.766,35.727,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Yanaka Reien (Cemetery) walk-through</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>After Yanaka Ginza · ~30 min · free · always open</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Tokyo's most atmospheric Edo-era cemetery. 100,000+ graves including the last Tokugawa shogun (Yoshinobu). Cherry-tree-lined central avenue (Sakura-dori). Cats live here. The cemetery is a public walking park as much as a graveyard — locals jog here.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The opposite of central Tokyo's chaos. Quiet, leafy, contemplative, distinctly old-Tokyo. 30 min through Sakura-dori, exits near Yanaka Ginza.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Tokugawa Yoshinobu's grave (last shogun, 1837-1913); the wide cherry-blossom avenue (May = leafy green, not pink); the cats that have lived here for generations.<br><br><b>↪ Connects Yanaka Ginza to Nippori station naturally</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7692,35.7269,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Kayaba Coffee (Yanaka lunch / coffee)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Open Tue ~08-18 · breakfast/lunch + coffee · cash + cc</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Historic Yanaka kissaten (coffee shop) in a 1916 wooden two-story house. Reopened 2009 after a restoration that preserved the original interior. Coffee, egg sandwiches, light lunch.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Lunch in a 110-year-old wooden Yanaka building. Egg sandwich is the canonical order. The room is the experience.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The 1916 building (one of the oldest still-operating Tokyo cafes); the egg sandwich (rumored Tokyo's best); the second-floor tatami room you can sit upstairs in.<br><br>Address: 6-1-29 Yanaka, Taito-ku · 5 min walk from Nezu or Sendagi<br><br><b>↪ Between Yanaka Ginza and the cemetery, walking route</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.772,35.7187,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>SCAI The Bathhouse (contemporary art in old bathhouse)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 12:00-18:00 (verify — closed some weekdays) · free · ~20 min</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Contemporary art gallery in a 200-year-old converted public bathhouse (sento). Shiraishi Contemporary Art's flagship. Rotating exhibitions of Japanese and international contemporary artists.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Old Tokyo + contemporary art crossover. The bathhouse building alone is worth the 20-min stop. Verify it's open Tuesday before going (closed some weekdays).<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The 200-year sento building (preserved exterior); Mariko Mori and other major Japanese contemporary artists have shown here; quietly one of Tokyo's most-respected small galleries.<br><br>Address: 6-1-23 Yanaka, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ 2 min from Kayaba Coffee</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.77,35.7176,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>InterContinental Tokyo Bay (HOTEL — afternoon decompress)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>~15:00-17:30 · rest before dinner</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Back to hotel for an afternoon decompress before the Shinjuku dinner. Tuesday is a foot-heavy morning (Tsukiji + Yanaka) — bank some energy.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Kokekokko is 19:00 in Shinjuku, 30 min from hotel. You'll want a shower + 60 min of rest before the dinner.<br><br><b>↪ From Yanaka: Chiyoda line Sendagi → Hibiya → Yurikamome to Takeshiba OR JR Nippori → Yamanote to Hamamatsuchō. ~40 min.</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7591,35.6549,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>BONUS — discovery zone</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tsukiji Hongan-ji (Indo-Saracenic Buddhist temple)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tsukiji-adjacent · free entry · ~15 min</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Pre-WWII Indo-Saracenic Buddhist temple (1934). Architecturally startling — Indian + Buddhist + Art Deco fusion in a Tokyo neighborhood that otherwise reads as fish market. Designed by Chuta Ito.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Gavin's pre-war architecture thread. 15-min walk-through worth doing while you're already at Tsukiji. Free.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Chuta Ito's 1934 masterwork; the Buddhist-Indian-Deco fusion is unique in Tokyo; small Buddhist-themed pipe organ inside (used for services).<br><br><b>↪ 3 min walk from Tsukiji Outer Market</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7715,35.6647,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Hama-rikyu Gardens (between Tsukiji and hotel)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>09:00-17:00 · ¥300 entry · 45-60 min</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> 18th-century shogunal duck-hunting estate. Saltwater pond fed by tidal channels, tea house on an island (Nakajima-no-Ochaya) serving matcha + wagashi for ¥850.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> On the walking route from Tsukiji back toward the hotel. Pre-modern Tokyo geometry surrounded by Shiodome high-rises. Tea-on-the-island is a Karolina moment.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The two 300-year-old pine trees; the saltwater pond tide-fed from Tokyo Bay; Nakajima tea house on the island.<br><br><b>↪ 10 min walk south from Tsukiji along the bay</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7634,35.6595,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Hagiso (converted-house art + cafe)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue ~11-21 · cafe + small gallery</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Yanaka art-space + cafe in a converted 1955 wooden apartment building. Ground floor: cafe with seasonal Japanese plates. Upstairs: rotating small art exhibitions, occasional shop pop-ups.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Quieter Yanaka alt to Kayaba Coffee. Modern-Yanaka aesthetic (converted-historic-building done thoughtfully). Light lunch alternative.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The 1955 apartment building preserved + adapted; the breakfast plate (asaichi) until 11; cafe-with-gallery model.<br><br><b>↪ 5 min walk from Yanaka Ginza</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7672,35.7245,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Niku no Suzuki menchi-katsu (Yanaka Ginza)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Yanaka Ginza · ~¥100 · standing snack</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Butcher shop on Yanaka Ginza famous for ¥100 menchi-katsu (deep-fried minced-beef cake). Locals eat them standing on the street.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Quick standing snack while walking Yanaka Ginza. The Yanaka-canonical street bite.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> ¥100 menchi-katsu; the line forms by midday; cash only.<br><br><b>↪ On Yanaka Ginza shopping street</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.766,35.727,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Nippori Fabric Town (Karolina pick if interested)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 10-19 most shops · ~90 min if you commit</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> A 1km stretch of Nippori between the station and Yanaka with 80+ fabric and sewing-notion shops. Tomato Fabric is the flagship — 5 floors of textiles at wholesale prices.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If Karolina sews or wants distinctive Japanese fabrics for any project — this is THE Tokyo destination. Otherwise skip.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Tomato Fabric (5 floors); the largest concentration of fabric shops in Tokyo; the 1km street layout.<br><br><b>↪ From Yanaka Ginza: 10 min walk east</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.773,35.729,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Asakura Museum of Sculpture (Yanaka)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 09:30-16:30 (closed Mon/Thu — verify Tue OK) · ¥500</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> The studio-residence of sculptor Fumio Asakura (1883-1964), preserved as a museum. Tatami rooms, a central pond, sculpture in every corner, a rooftop garden with city views.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If you want one small museum in Yanaka, this is it. The building itself is the attraction as much as the work.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Asakura's bronzes; the rooftop garden; the tatami studio with floor-level sculpture display.<br><br><b>↪ 5 min walk from Yanaka cemetery</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7672,35.7257,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Buying Guide (knives + audio + marine + scuba)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Reference — tap to read</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Full buying guide covering Kappabashi knives (which you bought today), Akihabara audio (which you saw today), marine optics (Fujinon stabilized binoculars at Yodobashi), and Tokyo scuba gear (Gull Mew fins at MIC21 Ginza).<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If you have follow-up purchases this week — audio gear in Tokyo Friday before flight, dive gear at MIC21 Ginza if you detour, etc.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Built up across Mon-Tue with research; covers all 4 categories.<br><br><b>↪ Tap the link below — opens https://gcmead.github.io/tokyo-tuesday-may26-map/BuyingAdvice.html</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.766,35.727,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>STATIONS — transit hubs</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Hamamatsuchō (JR Yamanote)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>hotel-side · 8 min walk</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> JR Yamanote loop. To Shimbashi 1 stop (then Hibiya to Tsukiji). To Shinjuku ~22 min westbound for Kokekokko.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7569,35.6553,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Tsukiji (Hibiya line)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tsukiji Outer Market arrival</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Hibiya line. From Shimbashi (1 stop from Hamamatsuchō) take Asakusa line 1 stop to Higashi-Ginza, transfer Hibiya 1 stop. Total ~22 min from hotel.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7706,35.6657,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Nippori (JR Yamanote + Keisei)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Yanaka entry station</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> JR Yamanote. From Hamamatsuchō ~25 min northbound. 5 min walk to Yanaka Ginza via the Yuyake Dandan stairs.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7711,35.728,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Sendagi (Chiyoda line)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Yanaka south entry</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Chiyoda subway. Drops you on the south side of Yanaka near Kayaba Coffee and SCAI The Bathhouse.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7656,35.7236,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Shinjuku (JR + multiple)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Kokekokko dinner</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> West exit + 7 min walk to Kokekokko. From hotel: Yamanote to Shinjuku ~22 min (or change at Shibuya).]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7006,35.6896,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

</Document>
